Document (dev.stxt.website): CMS and publishing

	Metadata:
		Author: ChatGPT 5.2
		Revision: Claude Fable 5
		Last modif: 2026-08-22

	Header: CMS and publishing

	Content >>
		A website, a blog, a book, the documentation of a product: every piece of content
		that gets published combines **narrative text** (sections, context), **structure**
		(chapters, blocks, relations) and **data** (status, dates, authors, tags). The
		tools that manage it —the CMSs— tend to couple several responsibilities:

		* data model,
		* editing interface,
		* validation rules,
		* and final presentation.

	Assert >>
		@STXT@ separates those layers with a **document format** that is stable, readable
		and reusable outside any particular tool: the publication is a text document, with
		validation, transformation and automation as optional steps.

	Subheader: What this case covers

	Content >>
		This case covers everything that is written to be published and goes through an
		editing and output workflow:

		* Corporate websites, blogs and content portals.
		* Articles, long posts and whitepapers.
		* Books and chapters.
		* Living technical documentation.
		* Public reports, catalogues and records.

	Subheader: Common problems

	Content >>
		The usual formats and tools have these limitations:

		* **Markdown**: flexible, but semantically poor; ad-hoc metadata in YAML front
		  matter; structure impossible to validate.
		* **Traditional CMSs**: rigid models that are hard to evolve; editing coupled to
		  one specific interface; hard to version and review; limited or proprietary
		  exports; strong dependency on the database.
		* **Editorial XML**: expressive, but verbose for manual editing.

		In all three cases the content is tied to the tool that manages it.

	Subheader: The @STXT@ approach

	Content >>
		@STXT@ acts as the **source format** of the content:

		* The CMS does not define the document format: it interprets it and offers tools
		  around it.
		* The content lives in files, not in the tool; it is versioned in Git and every
		  change is a clear textual diff, with no hidden state in a database.
		* One format for text, structure and data, with a separation between content and
		  rules (the templates).
		* Renderers are interchangeable: the same document comes out as web, PDF, EPUB,
		  feed or API. In a *headless* CMS, @STXT@ is the origin, the parser produces a
		  neutral tree and the front end consumes transformed data.

	Subheader: Example 1 — Web page

	Content >>
		A page of a site, with metadata, SEO and free content; the header and the body are
		`MARKDOWN` blocks, and the editor and the playground highlight them as Markdown. A
		CMS usually works with blocks or components as well; in @STXT@ they are nodes with
		a `Type` that the renderer transforms according to its own rules.

	Code >>
		Page (com.acme.cms.page):
			Title: About STXT
			Slug: about-stxt
			Language: en
			Status: Published
			Layout: default

			SEO:
				Title: STXT — Human First language
				Description: A readable, validatable text format
				Keywords:
					Keyword: stxt
					Keyword: documents
					Keyword: formats

			Header >>
				## STXT

				A semantic text language, readable by humans
				and reliable for machines.

			Content >>
				### What is STXT?

				STXT is a language designed for writing documents
				that can be read and processed without friction.

				### Principles

				- Human First
				- Minimal syntax
				- Optional validation

			Footer >>
				Last updated: January 2026

	Content >>
		Its template guarantees title, slug and status, controls the editorial states and
		leaves the rest optional:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.acme.cms.page
			Description >>
				Page: Standard CMS page
			Structure >>
				Page:
					Title: (1)
					Slug: (1)
					Language: (?) ENUM [es, en, fr]
					Status: (1) ENUM [Draft, Published, Archived]
					Layout: (?)

					SEO: (?)
						Title: (?) @Title
						Description: (?)
						Keywords: (?)
							Keyword: (+)

					Header: (?) MARKDOWN
					Content: (?) MARKDOWN
					Footer: (?) TEXT

	Subheader: Example 2 — Article

	Content >>
		An article with metadata, authors, status and long text. The text goes in `>>`
		text blocks —the body, of type `MARKDOWN`, contains Markdown that the @STXT@
		parser does not interpret—; the editorial structure —title, authors, status— is
		explicit.

	Code >>
		Article (com.acme.editorial.article):
			Title: Introduction to STXT
			Slug: introduction-to-stxt
			Authors:
				Author:
					Name: Joan Costa
					Role: Editor
			Published date: 2026-01-10
			Status: Draft
			Tags:
				Tag: stxt
				Tag: formats
				Tag: documentation

			Abstract >>
				STXT is a text language designed to balance
				human readability and machine reliability.

			Content >>
				## Why another format?

				Most existing formats force a choice:
				either they are comfortable for humans or reliable for machines.

				STXT tries to remove that dichotomy.

				## Principles

				- Human First
				- Minimal syntax
				- Optional validation
				- Security by design

	Content >>
		Its template ensures the minimum metadata and the editorial status, and does not
		constrain the text:

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.acme.editorial.article
			Description >>
				Article: Standard editorial article
			Structure >>
				Article:
					Title: (1)
					Slug: (1)
					Authors: (1)
						Author: (+)
							Name: (1)
							Role: (?)
					Published date: (?) DATE
					Status: (1) ENUM [Draft, Review, Published]
					Tags: (?)
						Tag: (+)

					Abstract: (?) TEXT
					Content: (1) MARKDOWN

	Subheader: Example 3 — Multi-section publication

	Content >>
		A book or a long document split into chapters follows the same pattern, with the
		structure visible and the text in blocks:

	Code >>
		Publication (com.acme.editorial.publication):
			Title: STXT — The book
			ISBN: 978-1-23456-789-0
			Language: en
			Status: In Progress

			Chapters:
				Chapter:
					Number: 1
					Title: Introduction
					Content >>
						This chapter introduces the basic concepts of STXT.

				Chapter:
					Number: 2
					Title: Syntax
					Content >>
						This chapter describes the syntax of the language
						with practical examples.

				Chapter:
					Number: 3
					Title: Validation
					Content >>
						Templates and Schemas add semantic rules.

	Subheader: The workflow

	Content >>
		A common workflow, in which every step is independent and replaceable:

		1. The author writes the @STXT@ document with any text editor.
		2. It is versioned in Git; review is a diff.
		3. A template validates the minimum structure (in the editor, on the command line
		   or in continuous integration).
		4. It is transformed to HTML, PDF, EPUB or other formats.
		5. It is published.

		The document contains no HTML, CSS or layout: presentation is decided in a later
		phase, and the same content can be rendered in several ways.

	Subheader: Practical advantages

	Content >>
		* **Visible structure**: an editor sees it without a graphical interface; a
		  developer maps nodes and blocks without guessing.
		* **Plain-text authoring**: no markup tags.
		* **Readable diffs** and review under version control.
		* **Validation**: the template rejects incomplete pages and articles.
		* **Automation**: indexes, tables of contents, feeds, APIs.
		* **Longevity**: the content does not depend on a specific tool.

	Subheader: Recommendations

	Content >>
		* Few document types, and a template for each one.
		* `TEXT` or `MARKDOWN` for the body and long sections; concise, stable metadata.
		* Simple blocks, with descriptive names.
		* Presentation outside the document, and no conditional logic in the content.
		* Templates that require the minimum structure and leave the rest optional.

	Subheader: Summary

	Content >>
		@STXT@ as the basis of a CMS and of editorial publishing gives:

		* Portable content: text stays text.
		* Explicit structure.
		* Optional validation.
		* Independence from tools and frameworks: the content evolves without changing
		  format.
